{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat task.md; cat history.md'","aggregated_output":"# Google QUEST Q&A Labeling\n\nLink: https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/google-quest-challenge  (overview, data and evaluation tabs)\nMetric direction: higher is better\n\n## Official description (overview + evaluation + data)\n(source: MLE-bench `description.md`, github.com/openai/mle-bench)\n\n# Overview\n## Description\nComputers are really good at answering questions with single, verifiable answers. But, humans are often still better at answering questions about opinions, recommendations, or personal experiences.\n\nHumans are better at addressing subjective questions that require a deeper, multidimensional understanding of context - something computers aren't trained to do well…yet.. Questions can take many forms - some have multi-sentence elaborations, others may be simple curiosity or a fully developed problem. They can have multiple intents, or seek advice and opinions. Some may be helpful and others interesting. Some are simple right or wrong.\n\n![Human computable dimensions](https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/competitions/google-research/human_computable_dimensions_1.png)\n\nUnfortunately, it’s hard to build better subjective question-answering algorithms because of a lack of data and predictive models. That’s why the [CrowdSource](https://crowdsource.google.com/csf/#/) team at Google Research, a group dedicated to advancing NLP and other types of ML science via crowdsourcing, has collected data on a number of these quality scoring aspects.\n\nIn this competition, you’re challenged to use this new dataset to build predictive algorithms for different subjective aspects of question-answering. The question-answer pairs were gathered from nearly 70 different websites, in a \"common-sense\" fashion. Our raters received minimal guidance and training, and relied largely on their subjective interpretation of the prompts. As such, each prompt was crafted in the most intuitive fashion so that raters could simply use their common-sense to complete the task. By lessening our dependency on complicated and opaque rating guidelines, we hope to increase the re-use value of this data set. What you see is what you get!\n\nDemonstrating these subjective labels can be predicted reliably can shine a new light on this research area. Results from this competition will inform the way future intelligent Q&A systems will get built, hopefully contributing to them becoming more human-like.\n\n## Evaluation\n\nSubmissions are evaluated on the mean column-wise [Spearman's correlation coefficient](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spearman%27s_rank_correlation_coefficient). The Spearman's rank correlation is computed for each target column, and the mean of these values is calculated for the submission score.\n\nFor each qa_id in the test set, you must predict a probability for each target variable. The predictions should be in the range [0,1]. The file should contain a header and have the following format:\n\n```\nqa_id,question_asker_intent_understanding,...,answer_well_written\n6,0.0,...,0.5\n8,0.5,...,0.1\n18,1.0,...,0.0\netc.\n```\n\n## Timeline\n\n- February 3, 2020 - Team Merger deadline. This is the last day participants may join or merge teams.\n- February 3, 2020 - Entry deadline. You must accept the competition rules before this date in order to compete.\n- February 10, 2020 - Final submission deadline.\n\nAll deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.\n\n## Prizes\n\n- 1st place - $10,000\n- 2nd place - $7,000\n- 3rd place - $5,000\n- 4th place - $2,000\n- 5th place - $1,000\n\n## Notebooks Requirements\n\nSubmissions to this competition must be made through Notebooks. Your Notebook will re-run automatically against an unseen test set, and needs to output a file named submission.csv. You can still train a model offline, upload it as a dataset, and use the notebook exclusively to perform inference.\n\nIn order for the \"Submit to Competition\" button to be active after a commit, the following conditions must be met:\n\n  - CPU Notebooks <= 9 hours run-time\n  - GPU Notebooks <= 2 hours run-time\n  - Internet must be turned off\n\nIn synchronous Notebooks-only competitions, note that a submission that results in an error--either within the notebook or within the process of scoring--will count against your daily submission limit and will not return the specific error message. This is to limit probing the private test set.\n\nPlease note - committing your Notebook will result in a full execution of your code from top-to-bottom.\n\nPlease see the [Code Competition FAQ](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#kernels-only-FAQ) for more information on how to submit.\n\n## Citation\n\nDanicky, Praveen Paritosh, Walter Reade, Addison Howard, Mark McDonald. (2019). Google QUEST Q&A Labeling. Kaggle. https://kaggle.com/competitions/google-quest-challenge\n\n# Dataset Description\n\nThe data for this competition includes questions and answers from various StackExchange properties. Your task is to predict target values of 30 labels for each question-answer pair.\n\nThe list of 30 target labels are the same as the column names in the `sample_submission.csv` file. Target labels with the prefix `question_` relate to the `question_title` and/or `question_body` features in the data. Target labels with the prefix `answer_` relate to the `answer` feature.\n\nEach row contains a single question and a single answer to that question, along with additional features. The training data contains rows with some duplicated questions (but with different answers). The test data does not contain any duplicated questions.\n\nThis is not a binary prediction challenge. Target labels are aggregated from multiple raters, and can have continuous values in the range `[0,1]`. Therefore, predictions must also be in that range.\n\nSince this is a synchronous re-run competition, you only have access to the Public test set. For planning purposes, the re-run test set is no larger than 10,000 rows, and less than 8 Mb uncompressed.\n\nAdditional information about the labels and collection method will be provided by the competition sponsor in the forum.\n\n## File descriptions\n\n  - **train.csv** - the training data (target labels are the last 30 columns)\n  - **test.csv** - the test set (you must predict 30 labels for each test set row)\n  - **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format; column names are the 30 target labels\n\n\n## Summary from our notes\nGoogle QUEST Q&A Labeling (Kaggle, 2019-20). For 6,079 StackExchange question-answer pairs (question_title, question_body, answer, plus category/host), predict 30 continuous quality labels (e.g. question_asker_intent _understanding, answer_helpful) that are averages of crowd ratings and take a few discrete values. Metric: mean Spearman correlation over the 30 columns, higher is better. Solutions fine-tune BERT-family models on question and answer text, and post-process predictions (rounding to the discrete levels) because Spearman depends on ranks.\n# Current version and earlier versions\n\nCurrent version: **v22**, leaderboard score 0.35115 (higher is better).\n\n## Earlier versions (oldest first; goal of the change, summary of the change, score the version reached)\nv1 -> v2: Finalize the inference script so it reads test and sample submission files, formats raw model outputs into the expected submission DataFrame (including qa_id), and writes submission.csv. | change: Added pandas reads for test.csv and sample_submission.csv near the top; removed a duplicated ROOT assignment; replaced printing the raw result with building a preds DataFrame using submission columns, concatenated qa_id from test, and saved submissio | score after: n/a\nv2 -> v3: Clip model predictions into a safe numeric range to avoid exact 0/1 probabilities (prevent numerical issues and improve log-loss/stability of submission). | change: Added a single line to clip prediction probabilities with np.clip(result, 0.00001, 0.999999) before building the submission dataframe. | score after: n/a\nv3 -> v4: Ensure prediction array has a proper float dtype before creating the submission DataFrame (and add an import likely intended for later scaling). | change: Added 'from sklearn.preprocessing import MinMaxScaler' import and converted prediction result to a float ndarray using np.asfarray(result, float) before building the submission DataFrame. | score after: n/a\nv4 -> v5: Adjust prediction post-processing to allow exact 0/1 probabilities and remove an unnecessary float cast. | change: Replaced clipping from [1e-5, 0.999999] and an explicit np.asfarray cast with a single np.clip(result, 0, 1) call. | score after: n/a\nv5 -> v6: Stop clipping the model outputs so the raw prediction values (probabilities/logits) are written to the submission instead of being forced into [0,1]. | change: Removed a single post-processing line that clipped prediction values (np.clip(result, 0, 1)); predictions are now used as-is when constructing the submission DataFrame. | score after: n/a\nv6 -> v7: Reduce noisy console output by removing non-essential logger.warning messages about GPU availability/configuration. | change: Removed two logger.warning calls that printed GPU availability and GPU count mismatch warnings, reducing verbosity. | score after: n/a\nv7 -> v8: Increase the inference DataLoader batch size to speed up prediction throughput (process more samples per GPU/CPU batch). | change: Single-line change: DataLoader batch_size increased from 4 to 8 in the test dataloader instantiation. | score after: n/a\nv8 -> v9: Ensure the prediction array is cast to float64 before building the submission DataFrame, likely to avoid dtype/compatibility issues during concatenation or CSV output. | change: Added a single line casting the prediction result array to np.float64: result = result.astype(np.float64) before creating the preds_df and concatenating into the submission. | score after: n/a\nv9 -> v10: Rescale model outputs to a 0–1 range per target and explicitly map them to the official target columns before writing the submission file (and tidy the submission-building flow). | change: Added an explicit target_cols list; applied column-wise min-max normalization to prediction array; moved reading of sample_submission to just before saving and assigned result to submission_df[target_cols]; added a small value display (result.min/ ma | score after: -0.00372\nv10 -> v11: Temporarily disable the per-column min-max normalization and inspect the raw prediction shape to debug/verify model outputs before creating the submission. | change: Replaced active per-column min-max normalization and min/max inspection with a call to show result.shape; the normalization and min/max lines were commented out. | score after: -0.00372 (+0.00000)\nv11 -> v12: No code changes — likely a re-run or checkpoint with no functional edits to verify results. | change: Empty diff; identical code (NOOP). | score after: -0.00372 (+0.00000)\nv12 -> v13: Use the training set label distribution to discretize continuous model outputs into the same discrete values seen in train, producing submission-ready integer/ordinal predictions. | change: Added loading of train.csv and replaced raw predictions with a per-column quantile-based mapping: for each target column, compute cumulative frequencies of discrete label values in train, then map prediction quantile ranges to those label values; wri | score after: -0.00372 (+0.00000)\nv13 -> v14: Increase the inference DataLoader batch size to speed up evaluation/throughput without changing model outputs. | change: Adjusted test DataLoader batch_size from 8 to 32 (single-line change). | score after: -0.00372 (+0.00000)\nv14 -> v15: Inspect the first element of the inference result in the notebook to verify outputs (no functional change). | change: Added a single expression `result[0]` to display the first prediction for inspection; no change to prediction logic or submission. | score after: -0.00372 (+0.00000)\nv15 -> v16: Use a newer pretrained checkpoint and allow longer input sequences while fixing input-concatenation so predictions better match the intended preprocessing. | change: Changed sentence construction from list concatenation to element-wise Series concatenation, increased max sequence length parameters from 256 to 512 (truncate_seq_pair and create_features), switched pretrained model path from /kaggle/input/bert-v1/ t | score after: -0.00372 (+0.00000)\nv16 -> v17: Disable the quantile-mapping post-processing and write raw model outputs to the submission file (use result instead of adjusted test_preds). | change: Commented-out the quantile-mapping post-processing block that adjusted predictions to match training quantiles, and changed assignment to the submission to use raw 'result' instead of 'test_preds'. | score after: -0.00372 (+0.00000)\nv17 -> v18: Switch to a different pretrained BERT checkpoint and enable lowercasing in the tokenizer so the tokenizer matches the uncased model and inference uses the new checkpoint. | change: Enabled lowercasing in BertTokenizer (False → True) in two places and changed model.from_pretrained path from '/kaggle/input/bert-v2/' to '/kaggle/input/bert-v3/'. | score after: -0.00372 (+0.00000)\nv18 -> v19: Use five-fold model averaging (ensemble of fold checkpoints) at inference to make predictions more robust and (hopefully) improve leaderboard score. | change: Added a shell copy of a config file, replaced single pretrained model loading with five fold-specific pretrained directories, created a Predictor per fold, collected each fold's predictions and averaged them, then assigned the averaged predictions to | score after: -0.00372 (+0.00000)\nv19 -> v20: Add a development-time sanity check to prevent silently writing a submission when the test set size is unexpectedly large (raise an error to catch the condition). | change: Inserted a conditional before writing submission.csv: if len(test_df) >= 1000 then raise ValueError('We'll never see this message again'), otherwise call submission_df.to_csv(...). | score after: n/a\nv20 -> v21: Fix the inference pipeline to read the test file correctly (use test variable and dynamic length) and remove the guard preventing writing the submission so a submission CSV is produced. | change: Removed a stray cell that copied a config file; changed read_data to read the test CSV (renamed variable and made targets length dynamic instead of fixed 952); commented out a ValueError guard so submission_df.to_csv is actually executed. | score after: 0.36763\nv21 -> v22: Temporarily disable the ensemble and run inference using only the fold4 checkpoint (likely to isolate/test that fold or reduce resource use). | change: Multiple model loads and predictor calls for folds 1,2,3,5 were commented out; only model4/predictor4 remains active. The submission assignment was changed to use result4 instead of the previous averaged ensemble predictions. | score after: 0.35115 (-0.01648)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
